WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:13.233
In this walkthrough we'll take a first tour of the workspace and learn
how to find anything. The screen has four parts — the top bar, the
navigation on the left, your workspace in the middle, and the agent on the right.

2
00:00:13.233 --> 00:00:23.933
Start on the left. The navigation lists every
screen, grouped by what it is for — trades,
instruments, market data, risk, and post-trade operations.

3
00:00:23.933 --> 00:00:34.567
On the right is the agent — a second way into the same
screens. Describe what you need in plain English and it
opens them, and the prompt library below has examples.

4
00:00:34.567 --> 00:00:42.233
Both sides fold away when you want
the room. The navigation collapses
from the button beside the Workbench name.

5
00:00:42.233 --> 00:00:48.367
The agent hides the same way, which gives
your workspace the full width of the screen.

6
00:00:48.367 --> 00:00:51.767
The same two buttons bring them back.

7
00:00:51.767 --> 00:00:55.233
Nothing is lost while they are hidden.

8
00:00:55.233 --> 00:01:03.033
Now the middle. Open a screen and it
appears as a panel in your workspace.
Panels are tiles rather than pop-up windows.

9
00:01:03.033 --> 00:01:12.467
Open a second and the two tile together, one
below the other. Neither hides the navigation
or the agent, so you keep your place while you work.

10
00:01:12.467 --> 00:01:19.833
Each panel has its own controls — maximise it,
detach it into a separate window, or close it.

11
00:01:19.833 --> 00:01:27.067
That leaves the top bar. Everything is shown
as of a business date, not today's date — the
first thing to check when a number looks wrong.

12
00:01:27.067 --> 00:01:37.200
Beside it is the role you are signed in as. It decides
what appears in the navigation, so if a colleague has an
item you do not, that is entitlements rather than a fault.

13
00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:46.700
An arrangement you like can be kept.
Templates hold saved layouts and presets for
common operations, so a morning setup is one click.

14
00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:51.867
Escape closes any menu or dialog,
and always backs you out safely.

15
00:01:51.867 --> 00:01:56.933
And when you have finished,
close every panel at once from here.

16
00:01:56.933 --> 00:02:04.633
One last thing, and the most useful. Command K,
or Control K, opens universal search from anywhere.

17
00:02:04.633 --> 00:02:12.200
Type what you are after and matching
screens come first. No need to
remember which menu group it lives under.

18
00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:18.200
It searches your data as well. An
ISIN finds the instrument itself.

19
00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:28.400
A counterparty name finds deals booked against
it. The same search covers users, parties and
market indexes, and a result opens straight away.

20
00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:35.533
And the top row hands what you typed to the
agent, so the same box is both a search and a question.

21
00:02:35.533 --> 00:02:45.233
That completes Getting Started in the Workbench. Every
surface shown here can also be opened from the agent
panel on the right, by asking for it in plain English.
